# Cursor Rules for Task-Driven Development

## Project Overview
This is a Next.js 15 application with TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, and App Router. We follow task-driven development principles with clear separation of concerns.

## Development Principles

### Task-Driven Development
- Break down features into small, manageable tasks
- Each task should be completable in 1-4 hours
- Create tasks in the `tasks/` directory with clear acceptance criteria
- Use the task template format consistently
- Link tasks to user stories and business requirements

### Code Organization
- Follow the App Router pattern with `src/app/` structure
- Use server components by default, client components only when needed
- Organize components by feature, not by type
- Keep business logic separate from UI components

### File Structure Standards
```
src/
├── app/                    # App Router pages and layouts
├── components/            # Reusable UI components
│   ├── ui/               # Base UI components (buttons, inputs, etc.)
│   └── features/         # Feature-specific components
├── lib/                  # Utility functions and configurations
├── hooks/                # Custom React hooks
├── types/                # TypeScript type definitions
├── styles/               # Global styles and theme
└── utils/                # Helper functions
```

## Coding Standards

### TypeScript
- Use strict mode and explicit types
- Prefer interfaces over types for object shapes
- Use generic types for reusable components
- Export types alongside components

### React/Next.js
- Use Server Components by default
- Add 'use client' directive only when necessary
- Prefer async/await over promises
- Use proper error boundaries
- Implement loading states and error handling

### Styling
- Use Tailwind CSS with consistent design tokens
- Create custom components for repeated patterns
- Use CSS modules for component-specific styles
- Follow mobile-first responsive design

### Performance
- Optimize images with Next.js Image component
- Use dynamic imports for code splitting
- Implement proper caching strategies
- Monitor bundle size and Core Web Vitals

## Task Management

### Task Structure
Each task should include:
- Clear title and description
- Acceptance criteria
- Dependencies
- Estimated effort
- Priority level
- Status tracking

### Branch Naming
- feature/task-name-brief-description
- bugfix/issue-description
- hotfix/critical-issue

### Commit Messages
- Use conventional commits format
- feat: new feature
- fix: bug fix  
- refactor: code refactoring
- docs: documentation changes
- test: adding tests
- chore: maintenance tasks

## Quality Assurance

### Code Review Checklist
- [ ] Follows TypeScript best practices
- [ ] Implements proper error handling
- [ ] Includes appropriate tests
- [ ] Follows accessibility guidelines
- [ ] Optimized for performance
- [ ] Consistent with design system

### CRUD Development Requirements
**When implementing CRUD functionality, ALWAYS verify:**
- [ ] Create operations work when clicking "Add" or "Create" buttons
- [ ] Read operations display data correctly in lists and detail views
- [ ] Update operations work when clicking "Edit" or "Save" buttons
- [ ] Delete operations work when clicking "Delete" or "Remove" buttons
- [ ] All UI buttons and forms are properly connected to API endpoints
- [ ] Error handling displays appropriate messages to users
- [ ] Success states provide clear feedback (notifications, redirects, etc.)
- [ ] Loading states are implemented during API calls
- [ ] Form validation works both client-side and server-side
- [ ] Data refreshes automatically after operations complete

### User Experience Requirements
**For optimal user experience, ALWAYS implement:**
- [ ] **Post-Creation Redirects**: After creating any entity (room, building, tenant, etc.), immediately redirect users to the created item's detail page
- [ ] **Real-time Notifications**: Display toast notifications for all CRUD operations:
  - Success: "Room created successfully", "Building updated", "Item deleted"
  - Errors: "Failed to create room", "Unable to update building"
  - Loading: "Creating room...", "Updating building..."
- [ ] **Consistent Notification System**: Use a centralized notification system across the entire application
- [ ] **Action Feedback**: Every user action should have immediate visual feedback
- [ ] **Optimistic Updates**: Where appropriate, update UI immediately and rollback on error
- [ ] **Navigation Flow**: Logical navigation patterns that guide users through workflows

### Testing Strategy
- Unit tests for utilities and hooks
- Integration tests for components
- E2E tests for critical user flows
- Visual regression tests for UI components
- **Manual testing of all CRUD UI interactions before considering feature complete**

## Security Guidelines
- Validate all user inputs
- Use environment variables for secrets
- Implement proper authentication/authorization
- Follow OWASP security guidelines
- Regular dependency updates
- **Note**: Environment files (.env*) are hidden from AI assistant view for security

## Documentation
- Document complex business logic
- Maintain up-to-date README
- Document API endpoints and data structures
- Keep task documentation current

## Deployment Workflow

### Production Environment
- **Hosting:** Hostinger Shared Hosting
- **Domain:** https://parenta.com.mx
- **Server:** Node.js 18.20.8 with PM2 process manager
- **Database:** Supabase PostgreSQL (external)
- **SSL:** Automatic HTTPS via LiteSpeed

### Deployment Process

#### Development Workflow
1. **Develop Locally:**
   ```bash
   npm run dev
   # Test at http://localhost:3030
   ```

2. **Test Changes:**
   - Test all CRUD operations
   - Verify authentication flows
   - Check responsive design
   - Test database connections
   - Ensure no console errors

3. **Commit to GitHub:**
   ```bash
   git add .
   git commit -m "feat: your feature description"
   git push origin main
   ```

4. **Deploy to Production:**
   ```bash
   ./scripts/deploy-with-manual-nodejs.sh
   ```

#### Automated Deployment Script
The deployment script performs these steps automatically:
1. Pushes latest changes to GitHub
2. Connects to Hostinger server via SSH
3. Pulls latest code from GitHub repository
4. Installs/updates dependencies on server
5. Builds application locally and uploads (to avoid server resource limits)
6. Restarts PM2 process manager
7. Verifies application is running

#### Deployment Methods

**Method 1: Automated Script (Recommended)**
```bash
cd /Users/adrianestopace/Documents/oneph/parenta-nextjs
./scripts/deploy-with-manual-nodejs.sh
```

**Method 2: Manual Deployment**
```bash
# 1. Build locally
npm run build

# 2. Upload + restart (uses SSH_PASS from scripts/.deploy-secrets)
./scripts/ssh-hostinger.sh connect
# Or: source scripts/deploy-env.sh && require_ssh_pass
# then rsync/ssh with "$SSH_PASS" — never commit passwords
```

**Method 3: Git-Based Deployment**
```bash
./scripts/deploy-git-auto.sh
```
This pulls code directly from GitHub on the server and builds there.

### Deployment Timing

**When to Deploy:**
- ✅ After completing a feature (all acceptance criteria met)
- ✅ After fixing a critical bug
- ✅ After testing locally and confirming everything works
- ✅ End of day with significant changes
- ✅ Before showing client/stakeholders new features

**When NOT to Deploy:**
- ❌ During active development (wait for feature completion)
- ❌ With failing tests or linter errors
- ❌ Without testing locally first
- ❌ With console errors or warnings
- ❌ During high-traffic hours (if possible)

### Post-Deployment Checklist
After deploying, ALWAYS verify:
- [ ] Application is accessible at https://parenta.com.mx
- [ ] PM2 status shows app is running: `pm2 status`
- [ ] No errors in logs: `pm2 logs parenta-app --lines 50`
- [ ] Login functionality works
- [ ] Database connections are working
- [ ] Recently changed features are working
- [ ] No console errors in browser

